# The second-sandbox preset ("Email" in the UI), injected the same way the
# SSH_MACHINES ones above are: only when this deployment actually configured
# one (see MAILBOX), so hosts without it simply never see the option.
#
# location is "host", NOT a new location string of its own, and that choice
# is deliberate twice over:
#
#  1. Nothing new to teach the unlock gate. _location_requires_unlock() is a
#     whitelist -- "container" and a recognized ssh:<machine> skip the gate,
#     everything else fails closed -- and _tier_for_location() falls back to
#     the strictest tier for anything it does not recognize. A brand-new
#     location would therefore demand a ROOT-tier passkey to read mail,
#     which is absurd; teaching both functions about it would mean editing
#     the two functions whose entire design is "do not edit me per preset".
#     Riding on "host" gets the host-tier passkey prompt, which is the right
#     answer here anyway: a stolen session cookie alone must not open
#     someone's mailbox.
#  2. No change to the sandbox CLI's privilege boundary. `sandbox exec` can
#     only address the persistent devbox container, so reaching a second one
#     "properly" would mean adding named-container exec to restricted-
#     sandbox -- the one file this host's own docs call the security
#     boundary itself. Running `sandbox new` as the pane's own command needs
#     none of that: it is the ordinary CLI, invoked exactly as a human would
#     from a host shell, under the sudo rule that already exists.
#
# The pane's command is the sandbox invocation itself rather than `bash -l`,
# so when Claude Code exits the pane exits with it -- this never leaves a
# host shell sitting behind the passkey gate.
if MAILBOX:
    TMUX_PRESETS["mail-tui"] = {
        # Flags BEFORE the `new` subcommand -- the CLI's real grammar is
        # "sandbox [-r|--root] [--claude [--fg]] <subcmd> ...". Its own
        # option loop deliberately stops at the first non-flag so that a
        # chat line passed to `exec` can contain something like "--root"
        # without being mistaken for a flag, which is why flags after the
        # subcommand are not accepted. (Measured 2026-10-04: `sandbox new
        # --claude mail DIR` silently takes "--claude" as the NAME, and
        # `sandbox new --fg ...` reaches an internal readlink as an
        # unrecognized option. Note that opt-tools.md and the sandbox
        # wrapper's own usage comment both still spell it "new --claude";
        # those are documentation errors, not a code bug.)
        #
        # AIGW_OUTBOX and AIGW_TALKTO: create_session sets them with
        # `env VAR=... <command>`, which puts them in the HOST pane, and
        # neither sudo nor `docker exec` carries them into the box (measured
        # 2026-10-04). Since 2026-10-09 create_session also passes
        # `--aigw-session <id>`, and the sandbox CLI sets exactly these
        # two inside the box from paths it builds under DIR/.aigw/<id>/ --
        # a narrow, validated option rather than forwarding env in general.
        # `box`, not `new` (2026-10-07, at the user's request): one resident
        # container box-<user>-<container_name> shared by every Email
        # session, the way devbox-<user> is shared, instead of a disposable
        # container per session under one fixed name -- which allowed only
        # one Email session at a time (a second died on docker's name
        # conflict). `box` also kills the session's own processes in the
        # container when the pane goes away (see the sandbox CLI).
        "command": ["sandbox", "--claude", "--fg", "box",
                    MAILBOX["container_name"], str(MAILBOX["dir"])],
        "location": "host",
        # Same 100 columns as claude-tui, same reason: the TUI lays itself
        # out to the pane width and the 220 default forces constant
        # horizontal scrolling in the screen panel on a phone.
        "width": 100,
        # No "remote_control": `sandbox --claude` builds its own claude
        # command line and already passes --remote-control itself, so there
        # is nothing for this file to inject.
    }

